  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 PM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nDry northwest flow aloft (PWs from 0.6 through 0.9 inches) and \nsurface ridging will be the main weather features through Friday. A \ndry and weak frontal boundary will move across the region late \nThursday. Areas of patchy fog is possible near area waterways almost \neach night. Temperatures will be 5 to 10 degrees above normal. \n\nFor this weekend into early next week, an upper trough over Texas \nwill pull tropical moisture northeastward. NHC still favors tropical \ndepression development with a 90 percent chance of formation over \nthe next couple of days. \n\nA good deal of uncertainty of the strength of low pressure \ndevelopment and path. Latest tropical advisories indicates a near \n100 percent chance of depressions within the next 48 hours. Latest \nGEFS tracks suggest a track into the Tennessee valley or southern \nAppalachians over the weekend.  Given the tropical-like moisture \nsome locally heavy rainfall is possible. \n\nAlso, depending on surface low track and expected surface wedge east \nof the Appalachians, tightening pressure gradients could produce \nstrong and gusty winds especially over the far east Tennessee \nmountains and foothills. Currently, the greatest threat of strong \nwinds will be late Saturday through Sunday morning. \n\nThis system will move east of the area by late Monday with upper \nridging building into the Tennessee valley and southern Appalachians \nfor Tuesday.",
